  "query_preview": "..\nThursday through Saturday: A critical fire weather pattern will\ncommence as an offshore upper low slowly approaches the Pacific\nNorthwest. Elevated instability (50-100 J/KG of 700-300mb \nMUCAPE) brings a 10-15% chance of isolated elevated showers and \nthunderstorms thunderstorms Thursday morning. Instability \nincreases and transitions to surface based by the afternoon, \nwith models showing 1000-1500 J/kg of SBCAPE over the Cascades \nand 500-1000 J/kg over northeast Washington, the Idaho \nPanhandle, and far southeast Washington. CAMs are showing \nstronger convection developing over the Cascades after 12PM, \nshifting into the Okanogan Highlands and northeast Washington \nthrough the afternoon. For southeast Washington, CAMs depict \nconvection firing around 1-2PM and moving to the northeast \nthrough the afternoon and evening. Inverted-V soundings due to \nhot and dry surface temperatures supports DCAPE above 1000 J/kg \nThursday afternoon, suggesting strong gusty outflow winds up \n40-50 mph will likely accompany any showers or thunderstorms. \nIncreasing onshore flow into western Washington will strengthen \nthe cross-Cascade pressure gradient Thursday afternoon, with \ngeneral winds through the Cascade gaps around 10 to 15 mph with \ngusts up 20 mph in the afternoon and evening. \n\nBy Friday into Saturday, an upper-level trough digs off the \nPacific Northwest coast from the Gulf of Alaska, pushing the \ninitial offshore low eastward, driving a dry cold front across \nthe region. Winds first increase on Friday as upper-level winds \nbegin to increase and mix down to the surface. Saturday \ncontinues to be the most impactful period of the forecast as the\nupper-level jet streak strengthens to 100-110 knots. Winds at \n850mb and 700mb increase to 25-40 knots, which combined with a \n+10mb PDX-GEG surface pressure gradient will support widespread \ngusty winds with southwest wind gusts 25 to 45 mph. On Friday \nthe NBM indicates the best chances for critical wind and \nrelative humidity combo to occur across the east slopes of the \nCascades, Waterville Plateau, Okanogan Valley, and Western \nColumbia Basin with sustained winds from the southwest 10 to 20 \nmph with gust 20 to 30 mph. Some cooling is expected on Saturday\n(highs in the upper 80s to low 90s), raising the afternoon \nrelative humidity, with a 40-70% chance for sustained winds \ngreater than 15 mph and relative humidity below 20% across much \nthe Inland Northwest. Despite the wetter RHs, the winds are \nexpected to be stronger than Friday (20-60% chance for gusts \ngreater than 45 mph), negating any benefit of the cooler \ntemperatures. Any new or existing fires Saturday will be capable\nof very rapid fire spread.\n\nSunday through Wednesday: Ensembles are in broad agreement for \nthe next upper-level trough to move onshore on Sunday, bringing \ncooler conditions, continued breezy winds, and a chance for \nlight preciptiation along the Cascade crest. The Gulf of Alaska \nwill remain active through at least the middle of next week with\nan amplified four corners high into the northern Rockies. \nOnshore flow into western Washington will continue with breezy \nand dry afternoon winds across the Inland Northwest. /vmt",
